# 1,2,3,4 — Procedural Causation and Closure

**Max Rupplin - MEARVK LLC - 2026.**

## 1. Base / Stem / Root Container

Establish the object, root medium, parent identity, representation, and entry
conditions before interpreting a detection.

## 2. Method / Incarnate Cause / Cause Again

Record the procedure that observes the object and retain successive causes when
transformations, dependencies, updates, extraction, or nested containers create
new observable states.

## 3. Attention / Final Medium Roots / Attenuation

Attention identifies what the procedure deliberately examines. Root continuity
connects the observed layer to its originating medium. Attenuation records
limits, uncertainty, unavailable content, and other conditions that reduce what
can legitimately be inferred.

## 4. Final Methods / Final Closures

Close only the procedure actually performed. A closure must preserve whether
content was fully observable, partially observable, or excluded by a limit.

### Institutional context

“Military actor” and “voting actor” are abstract institutional categories for
procedural records. They do not express political preference, evaluate a
candidate or party, or assign moral worth to people.

### Closure principle

> Preserve the causal chain; qualify the observation; do not convert absence of
> observation into proof of safety.

## Implementation

The experimental implementation is `libclamav/procedural_causation.c` with its
interface in `libclamav/procedural_causation.h`. The `.hss` and `.hsss` files
provide declarative specifications.
